behind their incisors and just put  in whatever liquid you need to put in  and they're pretty adaptable to that you  can see you don't have to restrain them  at all they're just sitting in their  little hutch and you put it in there is  something called liquid woodchuck diet  which is something kind of a thick  liquid kind of like pancake batter kind  of smells like pancake batter I've heard  it tastes like pancake batter but I  never actually tried it myself but the  woodchucks will really love this and you  can see the woodchuck on the right is  actually trying to pull the syringe away  from the researcher because he wants to  get all of it so they really like this  woodchuck diet so you can  as a carrier for any other liquid  medication if you need to or you can put  powders and mix it in because it's a  pretty thick liquid so that's another  good way to give them oral doses you can  do injections I am injections sub-q  injections but then of course we have to  handle the animal so we we tried to do  oral as much as possible and medical  issues some of our animals were wild  caught some were lab reared so we had  different issues depending on their  background wild-caught animals could  have parasites specifically we would see  coccidia they could have tapeworms if we  got them from a vendor they would  usually have already treated them for  tapeworms so we didn't often see that  but we did see coccidia they needed  several treatments sometimes to get rid  of those so that's one thing that we  would definitely treat right off the bat  they could come in with various kinds of  wounds or scars if it was an active  wound and open wound you can give them  antibiotics again the oral antibiotics  in some type of a liquid suspension is  the easiest way to do it for some of  these larger open wounds that are not  real deep but they just kind of like an  abrasion or a flat wound and we would  just put wonder dust on it this is an  antibiotic powder and it just kind of  helps dry the wound out and it also acts  you know as an antibiotic which it is  and we found that pretty successful  sometimes they would come in with the  Poteau dermatitis so the bottoms of  their feet would have ulcers on them  those are very hard to treat we  obviously would give them antibiotics  and try bandaging the feet just to kind  of give them some extra protection from  the stainless steel flooring we like to  use colloid bandage  which you can you know pick up at the  drugstore and that helps to keep the  area moist so they can heal but not not  too moist you don't want to you know  soaking wet and you can do the bandage  changes usually it's the back feet so  you can do the bandits changes in that  restrain air with their front end to  protect it and their back feet out where  you can reach them unfortunately they do  not tend to heal well the ones one that  we had was pretty bad when it came in  when we first got it this is a wild  caught animal and we never were able to  get it to heal basically this animal  lived in bandages the whole time we have  it and now once they're in the lab there  they can also get this pota dermatitis  and we did have one that develops well  we had it but he he cleared up pretty  well so I think if you catch it early  enough it's a lot easier to treat and we  put more bedding in his cage so that he  had a little more padding from the  floors antibiotics and he seemed to come  along quite well they can also get some  wounds even though we didn't you know  allow them to get together and fight  sometimes they would get themselves well  they could get out for one thing and  they could hurt themselves that way but  they would push their faces through the  door or then kind of scratch up their  noses sometimes and we had a number of  them develop something at the base of  the tail we're not really sure what it  was but because it was multiple animals  in the similar location we think it  might have been something maybe  physiologic in there it reminded me of  cats that have something called stud  tail it's a cumulation of a body of  secretions from the skin and it gets  kind of irritated you the hair gets lost  and that's kind of what these look like  they would lose their hair it would just  be a kind of large slightly ulcerated or  reddened area and we would treat those  mostly topically that was a good a good  way to use the wonder dust and again I  think it might have been something  physiologic hormone or something like  that but we never really could figure  out what was causing it didn't seem to  be from injury because it was at a place  on the body that doesn't really contact  much of anything in the research related  medical issues and there were several  especially in the hepatitis positive  animals because they could have liver  disease in addition some of the animals  were getting quite a few blood draws so  between a liver disease and a lot of  blood drives  we had some cases of anemia based on the  results of the blood tests  the CBC we could it looked like it was  an iron deficiency anemia  in these particular cases so we think it  was a combination anemia from liver  disease anemia from blood draws and  probably not enough iron in their diet  we don't really know exactly how much  iron they need in their diet but with  all the blood draws it seemed that they  were losing too much iron we would give  them some iron dextran and they would  come right around so that worked really  well they can have also clotting  disorders because their livers are not  creating enough clotting factors and  those we didn't see any clinical  problems with it but if we were doing  surgery we would give them some vitamin  K before surgery just to kind of give  them that little edge in case they had a  tendency to have a bleeding problem so  that was just something we did  prophylactically but we never saw any  clinical signs if you let the liver go  on to a more serious state they could  have other problems  low blood glucose seizures those those  can be problems as the livers get more  and more diseased we did that actually  need the animals to get to that point so  as soon as we thought they were starting  to get tumors we would we would usually  euthanize them at that point so they  didn't have to suffer the way we figured  out whether they were having problems  with by tracking their blood work  looking at liver enzymes and in  particular the GGT enzyme which I don't  remember what it stands for but it's GGT  that enzyme is very predictive of when  they get liver cancer so it would be you  know baseline baseline baseline and then  it would start to creep up and when we  saw creeping up we would watch them very  carefully  and it could go from you know our limit  of detection was about two it could go  up to fifty seventy a hundred or even  higher and at that point we were pretty  confident that they had liver tumor even  if they weren't showing any signs so at  that point we would usually euthanize  them because you know they we didn't  need them to be at that state so those  are kind of research related or whv  related diseases so that is pretty much  all I had to say about you know basic  care of the species and there's tons  more but I think I'll open it up to  questions and so that you know we can  see what specifically you're interested  in knowing so anyone has any questions  feel free to join in now well thank you  so very much doctor for your  presentation before we get started on  the question and answer session I would  like to remind our audience how to  submit questions you can submit  questions by typing them in the Q&amp;A box  which can be found by clicking on the  green Q&amp;A button at the low and left of  the presentation window we're going to  try to answer as many questions as we  can our first question for you is can  describe how you pair them for mating do  they injure each other during mating  well we actually did not mate animals  and we were not trying to breed them  they have we talked with other labs that  do mating and they don't really have a  problem with that they're generally  pretty affiliative the male's will seek  out the females and the females will  seek out the males so I have not heard  of them having any kind of injury from  mating they do only mate once a year so  and so they're only interested in each  other in the spring february-march if  they're outdoors in our next question is  where do you get the woodchucks well  that's a good question when we first  learned that we needed woodchucks they  were required by the FDA for this  particular study because there it's kind  of the model for HPV studies and we  looked into a number of options  including trapping them you know buying  them from a vendor trying to get them  from animal Patrol places that trapped  animals for a living and then maybe they  would give them to us one thing we found  out is that not all woodchuck  populations have woodchuck appetite as  virus so in our area which is  Connecticut most of the woodchucks do  not have this virus if you want to trap  animals in the wild you want to go to  parts of Pennsylvania New Jersey  Maryland a little further down on the  Atlantic seacoast and that's where it's  endemic in the population so we we  didn't really think we were going to do  that there is a vendor he actually works  out of Idaho but he comes east and he  traps woodchucks and takes them back and  he'll do the conditioning he will deworm  them he will you know take blood samples  you often need to know whether an animal  chronically infected or not so you want  to take multiple blood samples over a  period of time to be sure that they're  not just going through an acute phase of  the disease so he will do all of that  and then he'll sell them to you for a  large sum of money so that's that's an  option he there are not very many  woodchuck vendors out there that will do  that  so he's pretty much our only option  there was a vendor in Canada but we felt  that it would be impractical for us to  try to import the woodchucks across  country lines and it was easier to just  stay within the United States in one  case we were desperate for woodchucks  right away so he actually came directly  from his trapping run and gave us some  woodchucks that were wild caught that he  had just caught and had got conditions  them at all so we did have some wild  caught woodchucks  we had some trapped woodchucks and we  also had some woodchucks that had been  trapped as pregnant females which he had  taken back to his lab and then he would  raise the pups in the lab he could  actually really infect the pups manually  inject them with the woodchuck hepatitis  virus to increase the chances that they  will develop that chronic disease so you  can raise them that way and no increase  the percentage of them that will be  positive so we have had all three okay  and plastic before we let you go today  doctor is there any closing remarks  right now well I just I hope this was  interesting I hope it was useful to  people who might be getting woodchucks  in the future who have woodchucks now  and I hope it was at least interesting  to those who were just curious about  this species and I appreciate you all  coming to here thank you thank you  doctor again for your time in your  outstanding presentation I also like to  thank lab reeds for making today's  educational webcast possible today's  webcast will be available for on-demand  viewing through May 20  team you'll receive an email from  laberd's alerting you when this webcast  will be available for replay we invite  you to forward that announcement to your  colleagues who have may have missed  today's live event and until next time  goodbye
 

